<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Days – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Friday!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support. Interactive: Respond in a predictable pattern to simple questions about familiar things.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow with visual support the main plot of simple stories about the days of the week (e.g., "On Monday, I get ready for school."). Can listen and respond in a predictable pattern to simple questions about days of the week, including future references (e.g., "Today is Monday. What day is tomorrow?" "Tomorrow is Tuesday."). Can follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support by identifying familiar routines associated with specific days (e.g., "On Fridays, we go to the library.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Days of the Week" song (e.g., "Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, too...") using flashcards for each day. Students point to the corresponding day on a large classroom calendar as they sing. The teacher asks, "What day is today?" and models the response, "Today is Friday!" while pointing to the correct day.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards for "Wednesday" and "Friday," clearly pronouncing the words, emphasizing the /w/ sound in "Wednesday" and the /f/ sound in "Friday." The teacher then says, "Listen: F-F-F-Friday! W-W-W-Wednesday!" and has students repeat chorally. The teacher demonstrates pointing to the day on a calendar for "Today is..." and "Yesterday was..." with visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the days of the week.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'Monday' a day of the week?" (Students show thumbs up/down, or say "Yes!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'apple' a day of the week?" (Students show thumbs up/down, or say "No!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to Friday on the calendar) "What day is this?" (Students respond: "Friday!").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[5-7 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Days."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An animated short story or picture book with accompanying audio about a child's school week. For example, "A Week with Lily at School." Genre: Narrative excerpt (simple story). Register: Informal. Format: Audio with visual support (digital storybook or large flashcards/illustrations).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support.
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher plays the audio story. After each day's description, the teacher pauses and asks gist questions, encouraging pointing or simple predictable responses.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Listen for Monday!" (Plays audio: "On Monday, Lily has English class and draws pictures.") Teacher asks: "What does Lily do on Monday?" (Students listen and point to the "English class" or "drawing" picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher plays audio for Tuesday: "On Tuesday, Lily has math and reads books." Teacher asks: "Does Lily have math on Tuesday? Thumbs up or thumbs down!" (Students show thumbs up).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher plays audio for Friday: "On Friday, Lily shares stories with her friend and likes to play." Teacher asks: "On Friday, does Lily talk to her friend?" (Students nod or say "Yes!"). "Does she like Friday?" (Students nod or show a happy face).</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher focuses on vocabulary like: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, school, math, science, English, draw, read, write, share, friend.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[10-12 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Present simple ("Today is...", "On [day], I have..."), Past simple with "be" ("Yesterday was..."). Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, calendar, schedule, activity, school, friend, math, science, English, be, start, learn, read, write, draw, like. Pronunciation: /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday." Pragmatic: Answering questions about days; expressing preferences (listening to "I like Friday!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Point/Circle.</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures representing days of the week and different school activities. The teacher says sentences, and students listen and point or circle the correct picture. E.g., Teacher: "Today is Friday." Students point to "Friday." Teacher: "Yesterday was Thursday." Students point to "Thursday." Teacher: "On Wednesday, we have science." Students point to "Wednesday" and the "science class" picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Listen and Match.</b> The teacher plays short audio clips (or says sentences) describing a day and an activity, e.g., "On Monday, we learn English." Students have small picture cards of days and activities. They listen and place the "Monday" card next to the "English class" card. The teacher specifically includes "Wednesday" and "Friday" to reinforce /w/ and /f/ sounds. E.g., Teacher: "Listen carefully. Yesterday was Wednesday, and we read a story." Students listen and arrange "Wednesday" and "read a story" cards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listening Focus): Listen and Find.</b> Students work in pairs with a set of mixed-up day and activity flashcards. One student (A) listens to the teacher say a day and activity (e.g., "On Thursday, we have math!"). Student A then finds the "Thursday" card and the "math" card and shows them to their partner (B). Student B listens and checks if A found the correct cards. This is a listening check, not necessarily oral production from students.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[15-18 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Days."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher prepares a set of large sequential picture cards depicting a child's typical school week activities (e.g., Monday: math, Tuesday: science, Wednesday: English, Thursday: art, Friday: library). The teacher also has a separate set of day flashcards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Listening and Sequencing "My School Week".</b> The teacher narrates a simple story about a child's week, using the target vocabulary and grammar ("On Monday, I go to school and have math class. Yesterday was Thursday. Today is Friday!"). Students listen carefully and, with visual support, place the activity picture cards in the correct sequence on a large poster board or designated area as the teacher narrates. As the teacher names a day, students listen and place the correct "day" card next to the corresponding activity card.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students are listening to a narrative directly about school days and routines, reinforcing the "School Days" scenario and the "Today Is Friday!" theme by sequencing activities up to Friday. The teacher emphasizes, "And finally, today is Friday! We go to the library and read books!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension and non-verbal collaborative task completion. Students demonstrate comprehension by correctly sequencing the visual information based on what they hear.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[8-10 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to note students' ability to follow instructions and identify days/activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Show."</b> Each student receives a mini-schedule chart (a grid with blank spaces for days and activities) and small picture cards of days and activities. The teacher says 3-4 sentences (e.g., "On Monday, we have English. Yesterday was Thursday. Today is Friday, and we like to draw!"). Students listen to each sentence and place the correct day and activity picture card in the appropriate spot on their chart. The teacher can use WH-questions like "What is it?" (pointing to a card) to elicit simple responses or confirmations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify the correct day when heard (e.g., places "Monday" card for "On Monday...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify the correct activity when heard (e.g., places "drawing" card for "we like to draw!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds in a predictable pattern (pointing/placing cards) to simple questions about familiar things (days/activities).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday" by selecting correct flashcards.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows two emojis: happy face and sad face) "Did I listen well today? Show me a happy face or a sad face!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows "Friday" flashcard) "Can you say 'Friday'?" (Students attempt to repeat).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[7-8 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new day did you hear today that you liked?" (Students can point to a flashcard or say the word if able).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which school activity did you hear about that you like?" (Students point to a picture or mime).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the listening activities worked best for 2nd graders? The use of TPR and visual aids significantly helped students follow the story and associate activities with specific days. The pronunciation practice for /w/ and /f/ was engaging through repetition and pointing. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students might need more explicit 1-on-1 repetition of the target vocabulary before moving to more complex listening tasks. For students struggling to distinguish between similar-sounding words or days, incorporate more auditory discrimination games. Need to ensure the audio input is consistently clear and at an appropriate pace.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary for days of the week and associated school activities through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will use these same vocabulary words and concepts, but in a written format, allowing students to visually recognize the words they have now heard and comprehended, thus bridging the listening skill to reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[3-5 min]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>